nerated solutions often have a \"comb-like\" pattern separated by approximately \\( \\frac{2}{M} \\), which correlates with the reduction in overlaps for discrete Erd\u0151s-type problems.\n   - **Fallback**: If due to time limits, return the best stage-completed solution found.\n   ```\n\n   Remaining implementation details (function boundaries, full optimization cycles, etc.) would be unpacked similarly for brevity.\n   The key innovation is isolating combinatorial structure in the shift parameters while globally optimizing a few connectivity variables, reducing susceptibility to traditional gradient-based traps.",
